{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\": \"submission.json\", \"summary\": \"Submitted draft/submission.json (action=research) with the map update draft/map-v8.json as the new base (base version 7): a consolidation step after two frontier questions were answered in scope. The candidate behavior's persistence question is answered for the prefix that observed it — the two-leader overlap did not persist, because the second leader stepped down inside an eleven second window while the first still led the same term, and the review excluded a higher term, an unreachable peer and a transport failure, leaving the leader's own lease check — so that behavior no longer carries an open question; instead the question it raised is recorded, appropriately, on the lease behavior as a new unknown: whether the contact refresh interval used by a leader's own replication and heartbeat routines is coherent with LeaderLeaseTimeout for every configuration ValidateConfig accepts, together with the protection that the check still steps down a leader whose peers are unreachable, which is its purpose. The client-contract behavior's question is answered on its reachability half, because the second scenario showed that a request accepted while the node was leading can be left unresolved by a completed Shutdown, so only the choice of repair remains open there. The core overview rationale is corrected: where it previously described persistence as the remaining question on that line, it now records that the overlap did not persist in the observed prefix and that whether it can persist at all is a question about the lease rather than about the vote rule, while the status stays usable and no core gap is claimed. The submission carries four clarification map_changes and states that no requirement, identity, context or fact edge changed: the behaviors keep their owners, triggers, guards, reads, writes, branches, protections, produced and consumed facts and sources, and only two questions were resolved or narrowed and one unknown and protection were added. It also carries a handoff feedback that cites the window and accepted-request executions, records both answers, and lists the remaining leads: the lease coherence question, whether the overlap persists under a lease that covers the refresh interval, whether production TCP scheduling reproduces the heartbeat interleaving, which repair is intended for the accepted requests a Shutdown leaves unresolved, and the deferred snapshot commit-context surface. Optional preflight with the trusted validator returned valid=true; no target code was executed for this product, and the run now stands at six confirmed in-scope findings, one reviewed bounded negative, and a frontier whose strongest open lead is the lease's coherence with the contact refresh interval for configurations the library itself accepts.\"}"}}
